<file path="docs/owners/resources.md">
# Resources

Resources owns Repository-scoped physical Location, Equipment and Material identities. A physical
Resource has an explicit immutable kind, versioned name and description, an `ACTIVE | ARCHIVED`
lifecycle, immutable revisions and durable command receipts. Creation requires the kind; edit cannot
change it; archive and unarchive append revisions. Names, prose and units never infer physical kind.

The legacy Object, Object Type, quantity, relationship and engineering model is retired. Resources
does not publish compatibility contracts for it. Content remains the authority for work structure and
planned quantities; Execution remains the authority for actual reports and review.

## Public reference contract

The reference reader returns an exact physical Resource revision basis
`{resourceId, resourceRevisionId, resourceVersion, kind, name}` in caller order. New pins must match
the current Resource head and normally require an active Resource. A consumer may explicitly allow an
archived head; this does not permit arbitrary historical revisions. Existing frozen Content and
Execution pins retain their stored facts. Current Repository access is always rechecked.

## Authorization and consistency

- Current active User and Repository access are rechecked for reads, writes and receipt replay.
- WRITE, MAINTAIN or ADMIN may create, edit, archive and unarchive physical Resources.
- Permission is checked before replay. Authorized exact replay may return a committed revision after
  Repository archive; new mutations are rejected.
- Expected version, head, immutable revision and durable receipt commit or roll back in one
  Repository transaction.
- Reusing an actor/request identity with changed input conflicts.
- Runtime access to immutable revisions is read-only except when inserting the next committed
  revision. Runtime code does not perform DDL.

Repository owns enclosing access and archive policy. The neutral `@line_bot_v1/platform/text`
mechanism normalizes Resource name and description values; Resources retains identity, kind,
lifecycle, errors, transaction, revision and persistence authority.

## Mapping and verification

Runtime owner: `packages/resources`. Web consumes the physical Resource application and composition
contracts. Content and Execution consume the public reference reader; consumers never query
Resource-owned relations directly.

Verify domain transitions, current authorization, expected-version conflicts, exact replay,
transaction rollback, immutable revision reads and ordered current-head reference validation. Local
source, schema, native database, deployment and provider evidence remain separate.
</file>

<file path="docs/owners/wiki.md">
# Wiki

Wiki owns Repository-scoped page identity, immutable content revisions, page heads and durable exact-replay receipts. Repository remains the authority for scope, qualification, access and archive.

Wiki consumes the neutral [Platform](platform.md) `text` export for bounded title/body normalization, while retaining
raw Markdown meaning, validation timing, errors, immutable revisions and transaction authority.

Public reads, command receipts, replay and history use an explicit revision DTO projection. Application maps the internal Domain revision to the published field set; the Aggregate Repository exposes only Domain load/save with expected version. Read/qualification, transaction and durable receipt capabilities are output Ports; Application saves the head/revision and records its receipt in the same transaction. Command and Query models live in Application, and the concrete PostgreSQL adapter depends on scope/evidence rather than an Application command. Domain object identity and future internal fields are not part of the published contract.

- Each page has an independent version. Create expects version zero; subsequent commands require the observed page version.
- Create/edit supply title and raw Markdown body; rename changes only title. Archive preserves reads. Restore copies a same-page historical title/body into a new active revision.
- Reads and historical comparisons recheck current Repository access. Write requires current write/maintain/admin; Repository archive blocks new commands. Exact authorized replay may return its original receipt.
- Head, revision and receipt commit atomically; stale versions and reused request identities with changed content conflict. Reads never initialize pages.
- History uses version-keyset pages; page lists use stable page-ID cursors. Both have 20 items per page.
- Wiki does not own formal WBS scope, Issue execution, Markdown rendering, attachments or page-specific grants.

Delivery includes `GET/POST /api/repository-wiki` and Web collection/detail pages at `/{login}/{repository}/wiki` and `/wiki/{pageId}`. URL names locate current Repository scope; a write command must match the resolved Repository ID. The host verifies LINE identity and active Account mapping; owner queries/commands recheck current access. Web preserves exact unknown-result retries bound to User/Repository/page, clears private state on session loss/hidden views and renders Markdown only as escaped text. Machine facts: [semantic model](../../architecture/registry.json#domain), [module topology](../../architecture/registry.json#modules), [data topology](../../architecture/registry.json#data).
</file>

